\mt1 Readings of the Byzantine Text
\ip The New Testament of this edition is the King James Version conformed to the Byzantine text of the Greek New Testament as edited by Maurice A. Robinson and William G. Pierpont (2026). The King James translators followed the Received Text, the Greek printed by Erasmus, Stephanus and Beza, as F. H. A. Scrivener reconstructed it from their choices in 1894. The two Greek texts differ in some nineteen hundred places. Where the difference can be seen in English, the words of the King James Version are changed, as little as the Greek requires and in its own English, and a note marked TR gives the words it had; where the difference cannot be seen in English, nothing is changed. Four verses of the Received Text that the Byzantine manuscripts lack are omitted, each quoted in a note on the verse before it; the doxology of Romans stands at {ROM 14:24-26}, where those manuscripts have it; and {MAT 23:13} and {MAT 23:14} exchange places.\f + \ft The English changes are based on William G. Pierpont’s Some Improvements to the King James Version from the Majority Greek Manuscripts (1990). Where an instruction cannot be applied or its wording does not fit, a fitting parallel in the King James Version is preferred, then Pierpont’s wording, the Revised Version of 1881, and Robert Adam Boyd’s American Standard Version, Byzantine Text (2021). Boyd’s Text-Critical English New Testament (2022; © 2021 Robert Adam Boyd; CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/; source: https://archive.org/details/TCENT) and Graham G. Thomason’s Far Above All are occasionally consulted, in that order. Borrowed wording is adapted into the English of the King James Version. Robinson’s Greek texts and his collation of Scrivener with them, Boyd’s Text-Critical Greek New Testament (2022), the Majority Standard Bible, the World English Bible, the American Standard Version of 1901, and the CrossWire Bible Society’s tagging of the King James Version were also consulted.\f*
\ip This appendix lists every verse in which the two Greek texts differ or this edition corrects the King James English, book by book, under the number of this edition; a verse that the King James Bible numbers otherwise gives that number too. Each entry compares the Greek of the Received Text (TR) with that of the Byzantine text (RP) and, where the English changes, the King James Version (KJV) with this edition (OLEB). The English comparison also shows this edition’s corrections where the two Greek texts agree. Both texts use this edition’s systematic spelling and punctuation; separate revisions of a verse’s punctuation are excluded. Words the two share are printed once; the former reading stands in square brackets, followed by the new one, in bold in the Greek and in italics in the English. An ellipsis marks words left out; where a verse cannot be shortened, it is quoted whole. The Greek is Scrivener’s and the main text of RP2026, with their accents and punctuation; a reading of an apparatus that differs from it is labelled separately. The verses the Byzantine text lacks are given in full where the King James Bible has them.
{readings}
